      "math_academy_id": "q-101731",
      "sequence_position": 2,
      "problem": "Find an interval where the average rate of change of $f(x)$ equals $0$ given the following table of values of $f(x):$\n\n| $x$ | $\\,2\\,$ | $\\,4\\,$ | $\\,6\\,$ | $\\,8\\,$ | $\\,10\\,$ | $\\,12\\,$ | $\\,14\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $\\,4\\,$ | $\\,-3\\,$ | $\\,2\\,$ | $\\,6\\,$ | $\\,-4\\,$ | $\\,1\\,$ | $\\,6\\,$ |",
      "worked_solution": "We have $\\frac{Δf}{Δx}=0$ if and only if $Δf=0.$ So\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & Δf=0 \\\\  & f(b)-f(a)=0 \\\\  & f(b)=f(a).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means we have to find an interval  $[a,b]$ such that $f(a)=f(b).$\n\nSince $f(8)=f(14)=6,$ we conclude that $\\frac{Δf}{Δx}=0$ on the interval $[8,14].$",
      "answer_fields_complete": true,
